### Make paired history snapshots atomic (1/4)

**Severity:** `medium`

A render and a network sample can interleave between the separately synchronized histories, so the two chart arrays may briefly represent different sampling boundaries. Give each adapter one lock that protects both appends and both snapshots.

```suggestion
    internal sealed class AdapterHistory
    {
        private readonly Lock _lock = new();
        private readonly SampleHistory _sent;
        private readonly SampleHistory _received;

        public AdapterHistory(SampleHistory sent, SampleHistory received)
        {
            _sent = sent;
            _received = received;
        }

        public void Add(float sent, float received)
        {
            lock (_lock)
            {
                _sent.Add(sent);
                _received.Add(received);
            }
        }

        public (float[] Sent, float[] Received) Snapshot()
        {
            lock (_lock)
            {
                return (_sent.Snapshot(), _received.Snapshot());
            }
        }
    }
```

### Cover the paired history API (4/4)

**Severity:** `medium`

Add a deterministic regression test for the paired API and assert both arrays across multiple samples. This covers the paired sequence contract; it is not a concurrent-race reproduction.

```suggestion
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void GetHistory_ReturnsPairedSentAndReceivedSamples()
    {
        var stats = CreateNetworkStats();
        stats.ApplySnapshots([new(1, EthernetGuid, "Ethernet", 10_000, 2_000, 1_000_000)], 0);

        stats.ApplySnapshots([new(1, EthernetGuid, "Ethernet", 60_000, 27_000, 1_000_000)], 1);

        var (sent, received) = stats.GetHistory(1).Snapshot();
        CollectionAssert.AreEqual(new float[] { 0, 25_000 }, sent);
        CollectionAssert.AreEqual(new float[] { 0, 50_000 }, received);
    }
```
